# Line endings — this file exists to stop a specific, already-observed failure.
#
# Developers work on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, which checks these files
# out with CRLF. Copying one straight to the Jetson (scp/pscp, which is what the
# deploy path actually does) carries the CRLF with it, and bash dies on it:
#
#     syntax error near unexpected token `$'do\r''
#
# The service already running does NOT notice — it is already exec'd — so the
# boat looks perfectly healthy and fails on the NEXT restart or reboot. That is
# the worst possible time to discover it. Caught on 2026-09-03 in
# scripts/start_mavproxy.sh, before a restart, only because `bash -n` was run.
#
# Forcing LF in the working tree means a Windows checkout of these files is
# already what the Jetson needs, and a direct copy is safe.
#
# Python tolerates CRLF, which is exactly why this hid: the .py files were fine
# and only the shell scripts were broken. Pinning them too so the whole deploy
# path behaves the same way.

*.sh        text eol=lf
*.bash      text eol=lf
*.py        text eol=lf
*.service   text eol=lf
*.rules     text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf

# Build and interface files. rosidl parses .msg/.action LINE BY LINE, and colcon
# reads package.xml/CMakeLists.txt on the Jetson — none of them is a shell
# script, but all of them are files a Windows checkout hands to a Linux parser,
# which is the same class of bug. Pinned so the whole tree behaves one way
# rather than most of it.
*.msg       text eol=lf
*.action    text eol=lf
*.srv       text eol=lf
*.xml       text eol=lf
*.cfg       text eol=lf
*.launch    text eol=lf
CMakeLists.txt  text eol=lf

# Param dumps are read by param_guard/check_config on the Jetson.
*.params    text eol=lf
*.parm      text eol=lf

# Docs get read on the boat over SSH as often as on a laptop.
*.md        text eol=lf

# Binary — never touch these.
*.engine    binary
*.tlog      binary
*.raw       binary
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.pt        binary
